{
  "sub_unit_name": "Circular Queue",
  "subject_short_code": "DSA100",
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "What is a Circular Queue?",
      "description": "A Circular Queue is a queue in which the last position is connected back to the first position, forming a circular structure."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>A Circular Queue improves upon a standard linear queue by reusing empty spaces created after dequeue operations. Instead of moving elements, the queue wraps around to utilize available positions efficiently.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Why Circular?",
      "content": "The rear pointer can move back to the beginning of the array when it reaches the end, creating a circular flow."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Problem with Linear Queue"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>In a normal array-based queue, dequeued positions remain unused. Over time, this wastes memory even when empty spaces are available.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Linear Queue Memory Waste",
      "steps": [
        "Initial Queue: [10] [20] [30] [40] [50]",
        "Dequeue 10",
        "Dequeue 20",
        "[ ] [ ] [30] [40] [50]",
        "Front moves ahead",
        "Empty slots cannot be reused easily"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "warning-box",
      "title": "Limitation",
      "content": "Even though free space exists at the beginning of the array, new insertions may fail if the rear has reached the end."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Circular Queue Solution"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Circular Queue treats the array as a circle. When the rear reaches the last index, it wraps back to index 0 if free space is available.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Circular Structure",
      "steps": [
        "Index 0 → 1 → 2 → 3 → 4",
        "Last Index (4)",
        "Wrap Back",
        "Index 4 → 0"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Key Benefit",
      "content": "All available positions can be reused without shifting elements."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Queue Representation"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Circular Queue Layout",
      "steps": [
        "[10] [20] [30] [ ] [ ]",
        "Front = 0",
        "Rear = 2",
        "Elements stored sequentially"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Wrap Around Mechanism"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>The modulo operator (%) is used to move pointers back to the beginning after reaching the end of the array.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Circular Formula",
      "description": "Next Position = (Current Position + 1) % Capacity"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Rear Wrap Around Example",
      "steps": ["Capacity = 5", "Rear = 4", "(4 + 1) % 5", "Rear becomes 0"]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Enqueue Operation"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Insertion occurs at the rear. After insertion, the rear pointer advances using circular indexing.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "Enqueue Process",
      "steps": [
        "Check Queue Full",
        "Move Rear",
        "Insert Element",
        "Update Rear Position"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Enqueue Example",
      "steps": [
        "Queue: [10] [20] [30] [ ] [ ]",
        "Insert 40",
        "Rear Moves",
        "[10] [20] [30] [40] [ ]"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Enqueue Complexity",
      "time_complexity": "O(1)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Dequeue Operation"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>Deletion occurs from the front. After removal, the front pointer advances using circular indexing.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "process_flow",
      "title": "Dequeue Process",
      "steps": [
        "Check Queue Empty",
        "Remove Front Element",
        "Move Front Forward",
        "Update Queue"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Dequeue Example",
      "steps": [
        "[10] [20] [30] [40] [50]",
        "Remove 10",
        "Front Moves",
        "Front = 1"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "complexity-card",
      "title": "Dequeue Complexity",
      "time_complexity": "O(1)",
      "space_complexity": "O(1)"
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "How to Detect Empty Queue?"
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Empty Condition",
      "description": "A Circular Queue is empty when Front = -1."
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Empty Queue State",
      "steps": ["Front = -1", "Rear = -1", "Queue Contains No Elements"]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "How to Detect Full Queue?"
    },

    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<p>A Circular Queue becomes full when the next position of Rear equals Front.</p>"
    },

    {
      "type": "concept-card",
      "title": "Full Queue Formula",
      "description": "(Rear + 1) % Capacity == Front"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Full Queue Example",
      "steps": [
        "Capacity = 5",
        "Front = 2",
        "Rear = 1",
        "(1 + 1) % 5 = 2",
        "Queue Full"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Complete Circular Queue Walkthrough"
    },

    {
      "type": "diagram",
      "title": "Operation Sequence",
      "steps": [
        "Enqueue 10",
        "Enqueue 20",
        "Enqueue 30",
        "Dequeue 10",
        "Enqueue 40",
        "Enqueue 50",
        "Rear Wraps Around"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "fact-box",
      "title": "Observation",
      "content": "The queue continuously reuses free spaces without moving existing elements."
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Advantages of Circular Queue"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Benefits",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Efficient Memory Usage",
          "answer": "No unused spaces after dequeue operations."
        },
        {
          "question": "No Shifting Required",
          "answer": "Elements stay in place."
        },
        {
          "question": "Constant Time Operations",
          "answer": "Enqueue and Dequeue remain O(1)."
        },
        {
          "question": "Suitable for Fixed Buffers",
          "answer": "Common in hardware and networking systems."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Disadvantages of Circular Queue"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Limitations",
      "items": [
        {
          "question": "Fixed Capacity",
          "answer": "Size cannot grow automatically."
        },
        {
          "question": "Implementation Complexity",
          "answer": "Requires careful pointer management."
        },
        {
          "question": "Full Queue Detection",
          "answer": "Needs special conditions."
        }
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Circular Queue vs Linear Queue"
    },

    {
      "type": "comparison-card",
      "left_title": "Linear Queue",
      "right_title": "Circular Queue",
      "left_points": [
        "Memory waste possible",
        "Rear stops at end",
        "Simple implementation",
        "Less efficient"
      ],
      "right_points": [
        "Reuses memory",
        "Rear wraps around",
        "Better utilization",
        "More efficient"
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "table",
      "headers": ["Feature", "Linear Queue", "Circular Queue"],
      "rows": [
        ["Memory Utilization", "Moderate", "High"],
        ["Wrap Around", "No", "Yes"],
        ["Overflow Risk", "Higher", "Lower"],
        ["Space Efficiency", "Lower", "Higher"],
        ["Complexity", "Simple", "Moderate"]
      ]
    },

    {
      "type": "heading",
      "content": "Real-World Applications"
    },

    {
      "type": "accordion",
      "title": "Where Circular Queues Are Used",
      "items": [
        {
